<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">North Sea Canal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Noordzeekanaal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> Canal is a ship canal opened in 1876 that links <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> to the North Sea at IJmuiden, cutting through the dunes of North Holland. It replaced the slow route through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuiderzee">Zuiderzee</a> and the North Holland Canal, restoring Amsterdam's access for large ships and supporting its revival as a port and industrial city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-sea-canal.jpg" alt="North Sea Canal" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copernicus Sentinel-2, ESA · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amsterdam_with_North_Sea_Canal_by_Sentinel-2,_2018-06-30.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The North Holland Canal, built in 1824 to link the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, was long and narrow and soon too small for the growing traffic. A few decades later it was decided to dig a new canal across the narrowest point of Holland, the shortest route to the sea. Digging began on 8 March 1865 at the dunes of Breesaap. No <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> company was willing to take on the work, so it was awarded to an English contractor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The builders dug a channel through the old IJ Bay and lined it with dikes; the rest of the bay was then reclaimed and turned into polders, and a new cut was made through the dunes at Velsen. Nine side canals were dug so that the tributaries of the IJ, among them the Spaarne, the Zaan and the Nauerna Canal, could still drain and carry shipping. The work was done by hand, and the labourers lived in huts of twigs, driftwood, sod and straw, where disease, fights and heavy drinking were common.</p><p>At the eastern end the Oranje Locks were built in 1872, closing the IJ off from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuiderzee">Zuiderzee</a> so that a fixed water level could be kept. A small set of locks, the Zuidersluis, was built at the western mouth in 1876, where the new town of IJmuiden grew up. King William III opened the canal on 1 November 1876. The Middle Locks followed in 1896 and the North Locks in 1929, then the largest canal lock in the world, and the canal has been widened and deepened several times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The canal ends at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in the closed-off IJ, which joins the Amsterdam-Rhine Canal. Its water is drained to the sea through the sea lock at IJmuiden and the largest pumping station in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, a system vital to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> management of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Jetties built out into the sea, designed after research by the Waterloopkundig Laboratorium that began in 1957, keep the entrance from silting up. Tunnels for road, rail and metro, among them the IJ Tunnel and the Coen Tunnel, pass under it, and ferries cross it at Amsterdam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “North Sea Canal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Industry expanded from the late nineteenth century: the Nieuwe Waterweg of 1872 and the <b>North Sea Canal</b> of 1876 opened Rotterdam and Amsterdam to large ships, and firms such as Royal Dutch Shell, Philips and Unilever became multinational giants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
